From u8d1674b7b16af1b1 at groups.google.com  Sat Jan 26 20:14:41 2013
From: u8d1674b7b16af1b1 at groups.google.com (Dave Harris)
Date: Sat, 26 Jan 2013 20:14:41 +0000
Subject: Re: [D-RUG]: Extracting area from convex hulls & spatial autocorrelation packages
Message-ID: <7MQ2ALINHbU.FDmKyHOqA5UJ@davis-rug.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <7MQ2ALINHbU.q60OZhXjK-QJ@davis-rug.groups.google.com>
References: <7MQ2ALINHbU.q60OZhXjK-QJ@davis-rug.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/davis-rug/c/7MQ2ALINHbU/m/FDmKyHOqA5UJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Searching Google for "convex polygon area R" turns up this function <http://rss.acs.unt.edu/Rdoc/library/aspace/html/calc_mcp.html>.  It calculates a convex hull and automatically determines the area.  Looks like it has some nice features for discarding outliers as well.

Looks like there's also a straightforward way <http://mathworld.wolfram.com/PolygonArea.html> to calculate it directly using matrix techniques. Somewhat amusingly, it can calculate negative areas, depending on whether you go clockwise around the hull or counter-clockwise, but that's easy to fix.

I don't know anything about map projections, but I think you'd want to make sure you had an equal-area projection for something like this, to make sure that the area was reasonable.

Robert has some spatial autocorrelation stuff in the dismo package, but I'm not sure if it's related to what you're interested in.

Hope this helps!

Dave

